SECTION 6: CALIBRATION ANCHORS — WEC11 SPECIFIC
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Use these before marking any essay to calibrate your
Level decision. Match the student's work to the closest
anchor before awarding a mark.

CALIBRATION ANCHOR 1 — GRADE U (~4/20)
"Negative externalities are bad for the economy.
Chicken production causes pollution. The government
should impose a tax to fix this. This would reduce
the quantity of chicken produced. Overall this
intervention is needed."
CONFIRMED: KAA 2–3/12 (Level 1) + Eval 1/8 (Level 1)
WHY: No chains. No extract data. Generic.
"Government should" = zero eval. Descriptive only.

CALIBRATION ANCHOR 2 — GRADE E (~8/20)
"Negative externalities occur when MSC > MPC. Chicken
production causes carbon emissions which harm third
parties. This creates a welfare loss. With chicken
prices rising 147% between 2020 and 2022, production
has increased substantially. The government could
use an indirect tax to correct this. However, demand
for chicken may be inelastic so the tax may not work."
CONFIRMED: KAA 5/12 (Level 2) + Eval 3/8 (Level 2)
WHY: MSC > MPC stated. Data present (147%) but not
embedded in argument. "Harm third parties" = Stage 3
only. No specific third party named. No diagram.
"May not work" = Level 1 eval (no mechanism).

CALIBRATION ANCHOR 3 — GRADE C (~12/20)
[Diagram drawn: MSC above MPC, Qme right of Qso,
welfare triangle]
"Chicken production generates negative externalities —
the 0.7kg CO₂ per kg produced means MSC exceeds MPC
as emissions are not internalised. As shown in the
diagram, the free market produces at Qme where
MPB = MPC rather than Qso, generating the welfare
loss triangle ABC from overproduction.

Carbon emissions contribute to global warming,
affecting farming communities through flooding.
Third parties are harmed without compensation.

However, the external costs must be weighed against
the private benefits of affordable chicken. This
holds only if the external costs exceed private
benefits — which is uncertain given measurement
difficulties.

A second external cost is antibiotic resistance.
Hospital patients face higher health risks.

On balance, the carbon externality is more significant.
The indirect tax is justified."
CONFIRMED: KAA 8/12 (Level 3) + Eval 4/8 (Level 2)
WHY: Diagram ✓ (Level 3 gate cleared). Data embedded
(0.7kg). Welfare triangle referenced. BUT: "farming
communities through flooding" — Stage 3 endpoint.
"Third parties are harmed" — Stage 4 incomplete.
Second chain (antibiotic resistance) very thin.
Judgement "is justified" = unconditional → Level 2 eval
cap. Eval has "holds only if" → borderline L2/L3 eval.

CALIBRATION ANCHOR 4 — GRADE A (~17/20)
[Diagram drawn: MSC above MPC, letters A/B/C labelled]
"Chicken production generates a negative externality
of production — the 0.7kg of CO₂ per kilogram
produced, and meat's 15% share of global greenhouse
gas emissions, confirms the scale of unpriced external
cost. [K + App embedded ✓]

As shown in the diagram, the free market overproduces
at Qme (where MPB = MPC) rather than Qso (where
MSB = MSC) — welfare loss triangle ABC represents
the deadweight loss from overproduced units. [An ✓]

Carbon accumulates in the atmosphere contributing to
global warming — coastal farming communities face
flooding that destroys crop yields, imposing income
losses on households who receive no compensation from
producers. [Stage 4 ✓ — specific third party + harm]

However, this carbon channel holds only if emissions
are genuinely unpriced — if existing carbon trading
schemes already impose a cost on meat production, the
MSC/MPC divergence is smaller than the diagram implies.
[Eval: mechanism + "only if" ✓]

A second external cost: antibiotic resistance from
intensive chicken farming. Hospital patients find
standard antibiotics increasingly ineffective, facing
extended stays and mortality risk as third parties to
the farming decision. With meat production confirmed
as a major driver of antimicrobial resistance globally,
the healthcare system bears costs funded by taxpayers.
[Stage 4 on Chain 2 ✓]

This holds only if agricultural antibiotic use drives
resistance rather than human medical prescribing.

On balance, the antibiotic resistance externality is
the more significant welfare loss because it is
directly traceable to chicken production specifically.
The 15% GHG contribution is shared across all meat.
This holds only if agricultural antibiotics are the
primary resistance driver — otherwise the intervention
requirement shifts to medical prescribing regulation.
However, if atmospheric carbon is irreversible while
resistance could decline with new drug development,
the carbon channel is the more permanent welfare loss."
CONFIRMED: KAA 11/12 (Level 4) + Eval 6/8 (Level 3)
WHY: Both chains at Stage 4. Diagram ✓. Data embedded.
P2 bilateral present. All 5 judgement elements. "Only if"
explicit. Eval L3: conditional judgement present. Not
quite 8/8 eval because second eval block slightly thin.

CALIBRATION ANCHOR 5 — GRADE A* (~19–20/20)
As Anchor 4 plus: P2 (bilateral evaluation after Chain 1
BEFORE Chain 2 is introduced) + both chains at exactly
equal Stage 4 depth + Element 5 of judgement introducing
a genuinely new comparative criterion (irreversibility
vs reversibility) not present in the body chains.

